#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573975} :activity/id #uuid "93ccb3cd-e7d9-5748-946d-5b65c543fa11" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049174 :topic/id #uuid "e0ed8c88-7c9b-5941-8226-99cb93752f15" :topic/math-academy-id 3126} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fba15ffb-a809-59ae-b076-44ba54c1dd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364560} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc3fbb1-799a-58de-8cf0-3a963f9820d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364561} {:answer/id #uuid "c54d901d-18c6-5abf-9285-b92d09b6f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364562} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d7d203-577c-5220-b6b9-067e7b848250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364563} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf1411b-f278-502e-a927-3591272b4e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364564} :answer-field/id #uuid "2252911e-75f8-59ae-b664-6789cb01b1ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9ebe6e82-d911-58c0-a13c-cf64821873f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95107" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04ddc6fd-b21b-52ad-8cd2-7aed3b7a2fb9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22954aba-c66f-5f85-9e45-9aa6ce4d72bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404768} {:answer/id #uuid "f4383665-6278-580b-8943-01438ebff475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404769} {:answer/id #uuid "58794a90-ab7c-51d4-af96-b3bf9bf1f667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404770} {:answer/id #uuid "19eaeeb0-70cc-5efc-903b-969f1a50d985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404771} {:answer/id #uuid "42e7fff7-d8da-538d-9740-2d40d6c93c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404772}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404769} :answer-field/id #uuid "af751c8a-d34c-5ece-8db8-ee7406d5299f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d232faf-2de0-5a39-91fc-eea4fa2a2bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95069" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0e3c8864-c3d0-5194-8e33-93ae8cadd5bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc26c4b-5422-5f9d-b084-f9ac1cceab12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573476} {:answer/id #uuid "40df0e1a-c392-5ff6-927e-7576025abef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573502} {:answer/id #uuid "51914a47-fb5f-54c6-a62a-71498ce491cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573523} {:answer/id #uuid "7504d5c4-eb50-5ed5-b152-9db75043c19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573573} {:answer/id #uuid "92e8b72c-bdfe-5f38-b011-f271340c5d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573615} :answer-field/id #uuid "805c950a-cc3b-53ae-82fe-bf9e8c3510ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d75d5c4d-ca96-55cd-81ad-3a4ef4cad6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95096" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1a2e605c-b1b4-5c33-9bf9-2d4531698d99" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573969}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74508bdf-f140-5808-9753-25533e49826f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395005} {:answer/id #uuid "fda3000f-f50a-5453-b611-d8cc82671b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395006} {:answer/id #uuid "b2cb2171-7767-503d-acc5-5ef3cf65216f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395007} {:answer/id #uuid "34171141-8b0b-51c6-a150-b276b6fadb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395008} {:answer/id #uuid "c401f7c6-a855-53e8-a6f2-2c4c412ac54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395009} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3a3316-bf9a-5cf4-b520-c0a23f6216d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3b5dfdd-d519-5388-b5ca-0b0271c9801c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95100" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8ba26dcb-ada9-5956-bd40-70458277cdef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669f4399-98d0-58cb-b5ca-020a4654ee8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364546} {:answer/id #uuid "43a786aa-dee4-53e1-9bc6-44e46ac40c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364547} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f840fe-347c-55e1-bbf6-32e391995c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364548} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b0a414-8efb-5f7c-aa2d-597889961eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364549} {:answer/id #uuid "6efe3c1a-3df7-5ca6-9447-5ad51ed877d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and\t$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364550} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f96ff58-af7b-58d2-b1bc-1f8c4ba99615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a7869b9c-6fbb-54b1-92e1-8d96639ffbe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95065" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db57d3fb-c9bc-5778-9348-2f9f48a3eb8f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573951}}] :activity/title "Finding the Canonical Form of a Quadratic Form Using Orthogonal Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573417}]}
